        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 343,
        "headingId": 470,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-10T14:10:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 708,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 471,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108346,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 343,
        "headingId": 471,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Nasopharyngeal dermoid usually occurs in the nasopharynx, but it may arise in other areas of the pharynx {6668102; 10841951; 22413746; 23494895}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-10T14:10:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 709,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 472,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108347,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 343,
        "headingId": 472,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Babies and newborns may present with observable difficulties in breathing, swallowing, or eating due to an often pedunculated mass variably obstructing the nasopharynx {10841951; 6668102; 22413746}.</p>\r\n<h5>Imaging</h5>\r\n<p>A nasopharyngeal or oropharyngeal mass can be identified by various modalities {26001942; 30396417}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-10T14:10:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 710,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 473,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108348,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 343,
        "headingId": 473,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>About 7% of dermoids develop in the head and neck, with nasopharyngeal lesions representing about 10% of head and neck dermoids {30396417; 10841951; 6668102}. Most occur in infants and newborns {29019747; 15944568; 23494895; 8865778}, with girls affected more frequently than boys {25439551; 10841951; 22413746; 23494895}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-10T14:10:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 711,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 474,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108349,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 343,
        "headingId": 474,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Pharyngeal dermoids are developmental choristomatous anomalies, probably arising from first branchial cleft remnants along embryonic fusion lines {8865778; 30396417}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-10T14:10:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 712,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 475,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108350,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 343,
        "headingId": 475,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-10T14:10:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 719,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 482,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108351,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 343,
        "headingId": 482,
        "headingText": "<p>Grossly, nasopharyngeal dermoids are polypoid and covered with hair shafts, and grumous material may be seen. They may reach 120&nbsp;mm in size {8865778; 28247228; 22413746; 10841951}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-05-04T17:52:50"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 713,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 476,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108352,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 343,
        "headingId": 476,
        "headingText": "<p>Nasopharyngeal dermoids may be polypoid, solid, or cystic, showing stratified squamous epithelium associated with cutaneous adnexal structures such as hair follicles and sebaceous glands, sometimes creating a hairy polyp, and often with cartilage {8865778}. Teratoma, considered a separate entity, is diagnosed when all germ cell layers are represented.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-05-04T17:53:22"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 714,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 477,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108353,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 343,
        "headingId": 477,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-10T14:10:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 715,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 478,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108354,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 343,
        "headingId": 478,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-10T14:10:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 718,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 481,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108355,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 343,
        "headingId": 481,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> a polyp in the pharynx of squamous epithelium with associated adnexal structures (including cartilage).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-10T14:10:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 716,
